// Not supported on purpose: a `match` on a string that is used as a value and has no `_` or name arm. Sema
// accepts it, and when no arm matches the interpreter does not fail: it gives a unit value, so `f("c")` below
// prints an empty line. Native code cannot copy that for an `int`, so the emitter refuses. As a statement,
// without a catch-all, the same `match` is fine (28_string_match).
// expect-refusal: used as a value without a `_` or name arm
fn f(s: string) int {
    return match s {
        "a" => 1,
        "b" => 2,
    }
}

fn main() void {
    println(f("a"), f("b"))
}
